<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Look at the Fireworks! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> It's Festival Season.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Look at the Fireworks!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write a word or phrase to a group story or project with support or guidance. Interactive: Write simple messages to peers using words, drawings, and short phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a word or phrase to respond to a poll or questions about festivals (e.g., "Let's have a poll. Do you like festivals? Write yes or no."). Can write short messages about festival foods and activities collaboratively (e.g., "I eat cotton candy at the festival."). Can add a word or phrase to a group story with guidance (e.g., "The ______ are loud." "What is loud?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing flashcards of festival items (fireworks, cotton candy, music, dance) and says, "Look! What do you see?" Students identify them orally. The teacher then writes the word 'festival' on the board and asks, "What do we do at a festival?" inviting students to point to activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing simple descriptive words on the board next to the flashcards. For example, for 'fireworks', the teacher writes "bright" and "loud". For 'cotton candy', the teacher writes "sweet". The teacher pronounces the words clearly, emphasizing /f/ in "fireworks" and /m/ in "meal" (if shown). The teacher also writes "I like..." to model the start of a preference sentence. For 'party', the teacher points to 'P' and says /p/ as in 'party', then writes the letter P on the board.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are fireworks quiet or loud?" (Expected answer: Loud!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is cotton candy yummy or yucky?" (Expected answer: Yummy!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Material:</b> A simple, illustrated story or short descriptive text titled "My Festival Day." The text uses previously introduced vocabulary (festival, fireworks, cotton candy, music, fun, bright, loud) and target grammar (Subject + verb + adjective: "It is fun!", "They are bright!"; Present simple: "I eat cotton candy."). The text is presented on a large chart or projector.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"It's a festival! I am happy. I see colorful balloons. I watch bright fireworks. They are loud! I like the music. I eat cotton candy. It is fun!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Competence:</b> To express ideas, feelings, and emotions in written form using simple sentences about festivals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Comprehension tasks (Writing focus):</b> The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to the words. Students then get a worksheet with sentences from the story and matching pictures. They draw a line to match the written sentence to the correct picture (e.g., "I watch bright fireworks" to a picture of fireworks).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Alternatively, the teacher presents simple written True/False statements related to the text, and students circle "T" or "F" (e.g., "The fireworks are quiet. T / F").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing words and simple phrases using grammar (Subject + verb + adjective; Present simple) and vocabulary (festival, celebration, fireworks, meal, cotton candy, music, dance, fun, loud, bright, colorful, big, exciting). They will focus on identifying initial sounds like /p/ in "party" and linking them to writing. Pragmatic function: Describing favorite festival parts and asking/answering about festival foods.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Word Bank Fill-in-the-Blanks):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with simple sentences about festivals, each with a blank. Below the sentences, there is a small word bank with target vocabulary (e.g., *bright, loud, cotton candy, fun*). Students read the sentences and write the correct word from the word bank into each blank. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The fireworks are ______."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I eat ______ at the festival."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The festival is ______."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Writing (Sentence Completion with Choices):</b> The teacher displays a sentence starter on the board or provides a worksheet. Students choose from two or three visual options and write the corresponding word to complete the sentence. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of fireworks and a picture of music. Writes: "I like the ______." Students write either "fireworks" or "music."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows pictures of "happy" and "loud". Writes: "The celebration is ______." Students write "happy" or "loud."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher encourages students to say the word as they write it, focusing on initial sounds (e.g., /m/ for music).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Writing Simple Messages):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a small card with a drawing of a festival food or activity (e.g., cotton candy, dance, music). One student writes a simple message or question to their partner related to their picture, using words like "I like..." or "Do you like...?" (e.g., "I like cotton candy." or "Do you like music?"). The partner then writes a simple response (e.g., "Yes, I do!" or "No, I don't." or "I like dance."). The teacher circulates, checking for correct word spelling and simple sentence formation. This activity addresses writing simple messages to peers.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Fluency focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Task format:</b> Collaborative Group Festival Story / Poster.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher divides the class into small groups of 3-4 students. Each group receives a large sheet of paper or a small whiteboard to create a "Festival Story" or "My Favorite Festival Poster." The teacher provides sentence starters or picture prompts to guide their writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity:</b> The teacher writes a starting sentence on a large paper (e.g., "The festival is ______."). One student in each group writes an adjective (e.g., "fun," "happy," "exciting") to complete the sentence. Then, the teacher prompts, "What else do you see?" or "What do you eat?" Students collaboratively add words or short phrases to their group's paper, describing festival elements. For example, one student might draw fireworks and write "They are bright!" another might draw cotton candy and write "I eat cotton candy." The teacher encourages them to use the target vocabulary and grammar structures. The teacher provides support by asking guiding questions ("What is loud?" "What is colorful?"), helping them write the word or phrase.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This activity directly addresses the specific objective: "Write a word or phrase to a group story or project with support or guidance." Students also practice sociolinguistic competence by talking about and showing respect for different festivals as they contribute ideas.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the collaborative writing task and peer review of written messages.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Assessment Task:</b> Students' contributions to the group festival story/poster or their written messages to peers from the practice stage. The teacher collects the group papers or individual cards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility of written words/phrases.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (festival, fireworks, cotton candy, loud, bright, fun).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct spelling of practiced words/phrases (with minor errors tolerated for Pre-A1.4).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to add a word or phrase to a group story or write a simple message with guidance.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation and collaboration in the writing task.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket by circling a happy face or a sad face for "I can write words about festivals!" They also write one new word or phrase they learned today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What festival word did you like to write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you write about fireworks now? What word did you use?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to write single words and short phrases related to festivals, especially with visual and teacher support. The collaborative poster activity helped scaffold their writing. Some students struggled with letter formation for certain words, indicating a need for more phonics and tracing practice in future writing tasks. The use of pictures and sentence starters was effective for this grade level. Next time, I will ensure more individual written output opportunities for those who are ready.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their written contributions from this lesson to create a larger class festival book or presentation, sharing their descriptions and integrating all four skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>